{"name":"Sandblast","hex":"#decbab","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sandblast","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To spray with fast-moving solid grains (such as sand propelled by compressed air, although softer material like sodium bicarbonate used for delicate materials may also be so referred to). The process is used for stripping dirt, rust, paint etc. from the surface of objects."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Sand driven by a blast of air or steam for cutting and engraving figures on glass or metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sandblast"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sandblasting","blocks":[{"text":"Sandblasting, sometimes known as abrasive blasting, is the operation of forcibly propelling a stream of abrasive material against a surface under high pressure to smooth a rough surface, roughen a smooth surface, shape a surface or remove surface contaminants. A pressurised fluid, typically compressed air, or a centrifugal wheel is used to propel the blasting material. The first abrasive blasting process was patented by Benjamin Chew Tilghman on 18 October 1870."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Sandblasting1.jpg/330px-Sandblasting1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandblasting","wikidata":"Q917273"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Sandblasting3.jpg/500px-Sandblasting3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Sandblasting3.jpg/1280px-Sandblasting3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Sandblasting3.jpg/500px-Sandblasting3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Diesel powered compressor used as an air supply for sandblasting","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASandblasting3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Corrosion.Pit.jpg/500px-Corrosion.Pit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Corrosion.Pit.jpg/1280px-Corrosion.Pit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Corrosion.Pit.jpg/500px-Corrosion.Pit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A corrosion pit on the outside wall of a pipeline at a coating defect before and after abrasive blasting","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACorrosion.Pit.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/2018_Bead_blasting.jpg/500px-2018_Bead_blasting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/2018_Bead_blasting.jpg/500px-2018_Bead_blasting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Bead blasting paint from a concrete curb by a worker wearing hearing protection. Mixing particles with water substantially reduces dust.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2018_Bead_blasting.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/The_Employment_of_Women_in_Britain%2C_1914-1918_Q28375.jpg/500px-The_Employment_of_Women_in_Britain%2C_1914-1918_Q28375.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/The_Employment_of_Women_in_Britain%2C_1914-1918_Q28375.jpg/1280px-The_Employment_of_Women_in_Britain%2C_1914-1918_Q28375.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/The_Employment_of_Women_in_Britai